Collections.synchronizedMap() makes each single method call thread-safe, but iterating takes many calls in a row, so you have to lock manually around the whole loop.

1. What synchronizedMap does

It wraps a map so that every method (put, get, remove and so on) takes a lock first. Two threads can’t be inside those methods at the same moment.

java
Map<String, Integer> map = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());
map.put("a", 1);   // thread-safe
map.get("a");      // thread-safe
2. Why iteration is different

A for loop over a map isn’t one operation. It’s many small ones:

java
for (String key : map.keySet()) {   // 1. get iterator
    System.out.println(key);         // 2. next()... next()... next()...
}

Each next() call is safe by itself, but between those calls the lock is released. Another thread can sneak in and change the map in the gaps:

Thread A (looping):   next() ......... next() ......... next()
Thread B (writing):             put("x")  ← slips in between

When Thread A calls next() again, the iterator notices the map was modified behind its back and throws ConcurrentModificationException. In the worst case you could see inconsistent data instead of an exception.

3. The fix: lock the whole loop yourself

The documentation says you must synchronize on the wrapper map object while iterating:

java
Map<String, Integer> map = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());

synchronized (map) {                      // hold the lock for the whole loop
    for (String key : map.keySet()) {
        System.out.println(key + " = " + map.get(key));
    }
}

While Thread A is inside this synchronized (map) block, Thread B’s put has to wait, because it needs the same lock. When the loop finishes, B can continue. This is what “external synchronization” means: you write the synchronized block, because the map can’t do it for you.

You must lock on the same object the wrapper locks on, which is the map returned by synchronizedMap(...), not the original HashMap you passed in.

4. The downside

While one thread iterates, every other thread that touches the map waits. With a big map or a slow loop body, this becomes a bottleneck.

5. The better alternative

ConcurrentHashMap doesn’t have this problem. Its iterators are designed to work while other threads are writing, and they won’t throw ConcurrentModificationException:

java
Map<String, Integer> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

for (String key : map.keySet()) {   // no manual lock needed
    System.out.println(key);
}

The iterator is weakly consistent: it may or may not show changes made while you loop, but it won’t crash or corrupt anything.

6. Interview answer in one line

synchronizedMap locks each individual method call, but compound operations like iteration (and check-then-put) aren’t atomic, so you must wrap them in synchronized (map) { ... } yourself. ConcurrentHashMap avoids that.

The same applies to check-then-act code like if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v);. Two threads can both pass the check, so that also needs an external synchronized block (or putIfAbsent on a ConcurrentHashMap). That is a common follow-up question too.